  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the different reaction pathways of a copper-containing enzyme, separating outcomes for carbohydrate binding versus non-carbohydrate binding. Copper is shown cycling between Cu(II) and Cu(I), with reduction converting the oxidized copper center to the reduced form. In the upper, larger region labeled for non-carbohydrate binding, the reduced copper can react in several competing ways: as an oxidase, reducing oxygen to hydrogen peroxide; as a peroxidase, using hydrogen peroxide to generate radical species from a generic substrate; or through non-enzymatic oxidation of external reductants, again producing hydrogen peroxide. These side reactions appear to feed into an inactivation route, where reactive oxygen species accumulate and damage the enzyme.\n\nIn contrast, the lower region for carbohydrate binding shows more productive chemistry. When substrate is bound, the reduced copper center channels oxygen or hydrogen peroxide toward catalytic oxidation of the carbohydrate, illustrated as monooxygenase and peroxygenase activity, both yielding water as a byproduct. A dashed arrow suggests some transition or competition between these states. Overall, the diagram emphasizes that substrate binding steers the copper enzyme toward useful carbohydrate oxidation, whereas absence of substrate favors off-pathway oxidase/peroxidase chemistry and self-inactivation.",
